WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.967
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:08.967
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:16.795
Unit seventeen: Conflict Resolution. Today you will express outcomes with result clauses (so that, resulting in).

4
00:00:17.095 --> 00:00:19.771
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:23.966
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.817 --> 00:00:26.410
Conflict resolution.

7
00:00:27.417 --> 00:00:31.564
Conflict resolution means the process of ending a disagreement constructively.

8
00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:35.775
Conflict resolution training reduced disputes between departments.

9
00:00:36.383 --> 00:00:39.755
Your turn. Say: Conflict resolution.

10
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.054
Conflict resolution.

11
00:00:44.817 --> 00:00:45.864
Mediation.

12
00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:51.183
Mediation means intervention by a neutral third party to help settle a dispute.

13
00:00:51.783 --> 00:00:54.990
Mediation resolved the land dispute without a court case.

14
00:00:55.583 --> 00:00:57.829
Your turn. Say: Mediation.

15
00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:01.563
Mediation.

16
00:01:02.317 --> 00:01:03.562
Negotiation.

17
00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:07.769
Negotiation means discussion aimed at reaching an agreement.

18
00:01:08.383 --> 00:01:11.823
Negotiation lasted three days before both sides signed.

19
00:01:12.417 --> 00:01:15.835
Your turn. Say: Negotiation.

20
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:19.991
Negotiation.

21
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:21.995
Compromise.

22
00:01:22.983 --> 00:01:26.690
Compromise means an agreement in which each side gives up something.

23
00:01:27.283 --> 00:01:29.945
The compromise satisfied neither side completely.

24
00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:33.121
Your turn. Say: Compromise.

25
00:01:35.967 --> 00:01:37.072
Compromise.

26
00:01:37.817 --> 00:01:39.189
Win-win outcome.

27
00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:43.669
Win-win outcome means a result that benefits all parties.

28
00:01:44.283 --> 00:01:47.421
Sharing the water schedule created a win-win outcome.

29
00:01:48.017 --> 00:01:51.052
Your turn. Say: Win-win outcome.

30
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:55.447
Win-win outcome.

31
00:01:56.183 --> 00:01:57.510
De-escalation.

32
00:01:58.517 --> 00:02:01.863
De-escalation means reducing the intensity of a conflict.

33
00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:05.402
Calm language is the first step in de-escalation.

34
00:02:06.017 --> 00:02:09.284
Your turn. Say: De-escalation.

35
00:02:12.133 --> 00:02:13.378
De-escalation.

36
00:02:14.117 --> 00:02:15.257
Active listening.

37
00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:20.955
Active listening means fully concentrating on, understanding and reflecting what is said.

38
00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:24.258
Active listening made both farmers feel heard.

39
00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.537
Your turn. Say: Active listening.

40
00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:31.680
Active listening.

41
00:02:32.417 --> 00:02:33.569
Common ground.

42
00:02:34.583 --> 00:02:38.452
Common ground means shared interests or beliefs between opposing parties.

43
00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:41.978
Both villages found common ground in protecting the river.

44
00:02:42.583 --> 00:02:45.839
Your turn. Say: Common ground.

45
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:49.933
Common ground.

46
00:02:50.733 --> 00:02:54.663
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:55.517 --> 00:03:02.767
Visal, two neighbouring villages are fighting over irrigation water from the same canal. Shouldn't the authorities simply decide?

48
00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:11.353
An imposed decision might stop the argument temporarily, but it often results in lingering resentment and future conflict.

49
00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.171
So what's the alternative?

50
00:03:15.283 --> 00:03:26.771
A mediated dialogue in which each side explains its needs. Active listening can result in genuine common ground, such as a shared watering schedule.

51
00:03:27.883 --> 00:03:29.802
Will both villages accept it?

52
00:03:30.917 --> 00:03:40.698
They're more likely to, because they designed it. The process should be structured so that each side feels heard, resulting in a more durable agreement.

53
00:03:41.833 --> 00:03:43.357
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:44.217 --> 00:03:47.714
Grammar focus: Result clauses (so that, resulting in).

55
00:03:48.014 --> 00:03:49.363
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:53.048
Active listening can result in genuine common ground.

57
00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:57.145
The process was structured so that each side felt heard.

58
00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:01.547
Both sides compromised, resulting in a durable agreement.

59
00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:15.221
'Result in' introduces an effect; 'result from' introduces a cause. 'So that' expresses purpose with a clause (so that each side feels heard). ', resulting in + noun' adds the consequence of the whole previous clause.

60
00:04:15.983 --> 00:04:19.971
Be careful! Don't say: Listening can result to agreement.

61
00:04:21.933 --> 00:04:24.420
Say: Listening can result in agreement.

62
00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.244
Result in (effect).

63
00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:29.813
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.228
Positions and Interests.

65
00:04:32.528 --> 00:04:39.439
Conflict resolution theory distinguishes between the positions parties publicly adopt and the underlying interests that motivate them.

66
00:04:40.433 --> 00:04:49.921
In the classic example, two people who both demand an orange may discover that one wants the juice and the other the peel, resulting in a solution that satisfies both.

67
00:04:50.933 --> 00:04:58.134
Active listening is central to uncovering such interests, since people rarely reveal their deeper concerns until they feel understood.

68
00:04:59.133 --> 00:05:07.565
De-escalation techniques, such as acknowledging emotions and slowing the pace of discussion, create conditions so that rational problem-solving becomes possible.

69
00:05:08.567 --> 00:05:14.096
Agreements reached in this way tend to last, because each party recognises its own needs in the outcome.

70
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.213
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:23.094
Poor communication often results blank conflict.

72
00:05:23.394 --> 00:05:26.096
A, in. B, from. C, to.

73
00:05:29.633 --> 00:05:31.737
The answer is A: in.

74
00:05:32.883 --> 00:05:35.719
Intervention by a neutral third party is blank.

75
00:05:36.019 --> 00:05:40.288
A, negotiation. B, mediation. C, compromise.

76
00:05:43.833 --> 00:05:45.810
The answer is B: mediation.

77
00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:49.948
Shared interests between opposing parties are blank.

78
00:05:50.248 --> 00:05:55.132
A, de-escalation. B, win-win outcome. C, common ground.

79
00:05:58.667 --> 00:06:00.887
The answer is C: common ground.

80
00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:03.841
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:04.141 --> 00:06:14.075
A, They spoke slowly so that everyone understood.. B, They spoke slowly so that everyone understanding.. C, They spoke slowly resulting that everyone understood..

82
00:06:17.633 --> 00:06:21.804
The answer is A: They spoke slowly so that everyone understood..

83
00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:26.134
According to the reading, in the orange example the solution…

84
00:06:26.434 --> 00:06:32.165
A, satisfied both people. B, satisfied neither person. C, required a judge.

85
00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:38.396
The answer is A: satisfied both people.

86
00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:42.798
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:46.787
What role does active listening play in resolving a conflict?

88
00:07:03.583 --> 00:07:05.420
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:44.371
Active listening plays a central role because conflicts often persist when people feel unheard rather than when their demands are incompatible. By paraphrasing and acknowledging emotions, a listener lowers defensiveness, resulting in calmer and more honest discussion. It also reveals underlying interests behind rigid positions. For example, a dispute over a factory's noise may really concern children's sleep, which could be solved by changing shift times. Active listening transforms opponents into problem-solvers.

90
00:07:44.671 --> 00:07:46.346
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:52.143
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and result clauses (so that, resulting in).

92
00:07:52.443 --> 00:07:55.931
Today's value is empathy. Think about how others feel.

93
00:07:56.231 --> 00:07:58.826
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:59.126 --> 00:08:01.894
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

